  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/e/ec/Fantastic_Four_Vol_1_24.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The first couple of pages are spent talking to reporters from <i>Life</i> magazine, who are doing a cover story on the FF, with them salivating over Sue Storm, while Ben keeps trying to get their attention with feats of strength.&nbsp; But soon, a commotion at Times Square prompts the reporters out to investigate.\n\nWhen the Fantastic Four arrive, they begin to encounter very odd obstacles, which they seem easily able to overcome, making Reed suggest that whoever is responsible doesn't really know them, or surely the obstacles would be more formidable.&nbsp; Neither the Human Torch nor the Thing seems to have any effect on the mysterious force plaguing the downtown, and it isn't until Reed decides to use <i>noise</i> in the form of wailing police sirens to disrupt whatever it is.\n\nPage 7 finally reveals who is responsible, but he/it doesn't seem interested in causing any real harm.&nbsp; Even when he accidentally calls down a meteor storm onto them from above, it appears that he doesn't actually understand what he's doing, and this leads Reed to surmise that he's an <i>infant</i>!&nbsp; Reed suggests to the authorities that they essentially leave him alone.&nbsp; However, as the \"French have an expression called <i>enfant terrible</i>,\" he worries about what this \"infant terrible\" might do, as it seems he can do pretty much <i>anything</i> that pops into his head!\n\nReed discusses the Infant Terrible with his partners and realizes that if he decides to move the sun closer to the Earth to have a look, it's doomed.&nbsp; He tells the others to go back after him.&nbsp; The others balk at Reed staying behind, but they go.\n\nIn the meantime, a mob character named Big Joe sees a great deal of use for one with such amazing abilities, and he soon coaxes this infant to join him and his boys!&nbsp; We see an armored car floating through the sky to where the crooks wait, all after the Infant Terrible was shown pictures of what they wanted him to do.&nbsp; One of them even says, \"It's like manna from heaven!\"&nbsp; But as they reach for the loot, some of it turns into pigs, some sprouts wings and flies away, some even turns to mud!&nbsp; When Big Joe presses the infant to increase the size of a small diamond, it temporarily works but then turns into an egg and hatches.&nbsp; Big Joe is <i>not </i>amused, but the infant simply doesn't understand.\n\nWhile Reed contemplates the problem at hand, the other three members of the FF finally arrive at the place where the floating armored car landed, and they make short work of Big Joe's boys.&nbsp; However, in the process, they scare the Infant Terrible, and he soon leaves to create ever-more-horrible obstacles for them and even starts to have a temper tantrum that threatens all of New York City!\n\nBack at HQ, Reed spots a ship in space near Earth and sends it a message!&nbsp; The Infant Terrible's rampage causes havoc everywhere.&nbsp; Even Alicia is caught in the middle of it and is endangered by the surrounding crowd of panicked bystanders.\n\nBut -- <i>thank heaven!</i> -- the infant's parents finally show up and take back their child, who had just wandered off.&nbsp; Reed's message went through, and he gambled that anyone as powerful as <i>they</i> obviously were would respect their civilization enough to leave it in peace, despite their ability to destroy it with nothing more than a thought.\n\nThe day is saved.",
  "grade_one_liner": "Reed feared the Infant Terrible might destroy the solar system.\nI feared there were still ten pages left...",
  "subtitle": "The Issue Terrible",
  "review_text": "A foe that throws up seemingly-ridiculous obstacles reminds readers immediately of the Impossible Man's battle with the title heroes from back in [LINK:1962:FF_11_ff1|FF #11ff|2026-05-01] (second story).&nbsp; It has the same sort of \"cartoonish\" flavor to it.\n[ASSET: center | CrazyMenaces | width=67%]\n\nThe menace is not revealed until page 7, and the comparison to the aforementioned Impossible Man is hard to ignore, <i>particularly</i> since both of them are green and purple!\n\n[ASSET: left | MenaceRevealed | width=14%] [ASSET: right | OnlyAnInfant | width=22%] The action was <i>completely</i> haphazard, with Jack pretty much letting his imagination run wild, filling up panel after panel with mind-numbing conflict that seemed designed only to fill the page count ([SPAN: severity=info; text=and test the patience of every reader as <i>well</i>]).\n[BR]\n[BR] Only after the book has nearly reached its halfway point does Reed finally deduce that the creature's randomness is due to him being an \"infant!\"\n\nAt that point, \"Big Joe\" enters the scene, but by then the story had already exhausted my patience.\n[BR] All I could think was:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Big Deal!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I was even tempted to toss the book down in disgust!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=The co-creators then doubled down on the stupidity.]\n\nEven though Reed informs the others how dangerous this \"Infant Terrible\" really is -- that he could actually destroy the entire solar system with nary a thought (page 11) -- it is all done in the relative safety of their headquarters, having left the problem child out roaming the streets!\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=In other words, Reed recognizes the danger completely and then responds to it as casually as possible.]\n\n[ASSET: left | CandyAndIceCream | width=14%] This leaves the alien open to other influences, even to the point of being lured in by the mob -- led by a guy named \"Big Joe\" -- who sends a few of his \"boys\" out to entice this creature by offering him candy and ice cream.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Ugh!]&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=small; text=(There really was little hope for this issue now.)]\n\nThe Molecule Man that debuted in [LINK:1963:FF_20|FF #20|2026-12-25] could essentially conjure up whatever popped into artist Jack Kirby's brain, and this creature seemed built on much the same principle.\n\n[ASSET: right | ThisIsntFunny | width=11%] The armored car robbery that spanned pages 12 and 13 served no other purpose than to allow the Infant Terrible to misinterpret every prompt that \"Big Joe\" left him with.\n[BR]\n[BR] At least Big Joe raging at the alien and threatening him with guns on page 14, shouting out, \"This isn't funny!\" was accurate!\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; text=Indeed, there was almost <i>nothing</i> funny about this issue!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe last several pages couldn't pass by quickly enough, and the three other members of the FF (and even Alicia) managed to suck up the final third of the book, giving Reed time to contact the lost child's alien parents, who come to retrieve him.\n[ASSET: center | BackWithHisParents | width=67%]\n\nThe [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved.]\n[BR] But all I could think was... [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Thank God it's <i>over</i>!]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n[SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=About as bad an issue of <i>Fantastic Four</i> as I've ever encountered up to this point.]\n\nIt was as if Stan said to Jack, \"Okay Jack, create this lost infant who wanders to Earth, creating havoc, and use your imagination to come up with page-filling fight scenes until Reed finds its parents in space and calls them to pick up their kid and save the day, and I'll write a story around it.\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=I'm telling you:&nbsp; they must have had no time at all to come up with anything better.]\n\nYou might ask why a story like this didn't strike me the same as the similarly-powered Impossible Man did back in [LINK:1962:FF_11_ff1|FF #11|2026-05-01]'s second story (one where I suggested that the story was good, <i>provided</i> you were in the right mood for it).\n\nMy answer is that at <i>least</i> the Impossible Man had personality (ridiculous though it was).&nbsp; He was annoying, but he was <i>memorable</i>.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=This creature had neither quality.]\n\nFurthermore, for a television station to suggest (as we see at the bottom of page 9) that ordinary citizens simply \"do nothing to antagonize him!\" is about as silly a prospect as any reader can imagine (and shows how bankrupt this plot really was).\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=What a joke!]\n\n[ASSET: left | ImTheBoss | width=22%] [ASSET: right | InsultMyIntegrity | width=29%] But even in the midst of this [SPAN: severity=error; text=train wreck of a story,] there were two genuinely positive moments.\n[BR]\n[BR] The first came on page 12 where the Thing storms off in disgust after Reed orders the group to pipe down and let him think.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=It was a character-defining moment that showed the contrast between Ben's eagerness to leap into action fists first and Reed's analytical care.]\n[BR]\n[BR] And the second was when Big Joe offered the Thing an entire <b>grand</b> if he would only let him go, and Ben's outcry of insulted disgust caused me to laugh out loud.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Apparently a grand wasn't sufficient, but if he'd only <i>sweeten </i>the offer... <i>heh!</i>)]\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; text= But these were the only flashes of pleasure I got while reading this story.]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Stan's dialogue triumphs even when the plot calls in sick!)]\n\nWhile I <i>did</i> appreciate these, they were only humorous \"interruptions\" in my ever-growing disgust.&nbsp; It's a shame that a mag with as much promise as the FF, which has had <i>really</i> solid stories, tosses out the occasional [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=<i>epic</i> fail] like this -- one that <i>surely</i> would have fared much worse [SPAN: severity=info; text=if Ben Grimm hadn't showed up with a bucket and managed to save it from drowning...]\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=Don't waste your time reading it -- you're not missing <i>anything</i>!]",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] On page 4, Johnny comments on how well he hurled his fire ball at his target, mentioning that it was a \"strikeout!&nbsp; They should have me pitching for the <i>Mets</i>!!\"&nbsp; This is the first mention of the Mets that I have tracked, who were woefully inept at this point (1963).&nbsp; This book would have been plotted right at the end of only their second season of existence, and the Mets had managed to [SPAN: severity=error; text=lose over 100 games in both years], so I guess Johnny figured they needed all the help they could <i>get</i>!\n[BR]\n[BR] For the record:&nbsp; The Mets would go on to lose more than 100 games for another two years before they finally managed to lose <i>only</i> 95 games in 1966!\n\n[HANG0] The second panel on page 12 shows Ben grabbing Reed's outstretched [SPAN: severity=info; text=left] arm, but you can clearly see a [SPAN: severity=info; text=right-handed glove] lying on the floor.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Oops!]\n\n[HANG0] Page 18 has Johnny cutting a \"wide <b>swathe</b> on the ground\" in front of a large rock creature created by the Infant Terrible.&nbsp; While \"swathe\" is an actual word, I don't think it was the one Stan intended.&nbsp; \"<b>Swath</b>\" is likely the one he had in mind."
  ],
  "letters_page": "John F. Lebar's printed letter made me laugh when he suggested a Marvel \"gossip column\" with taglines such as \"Is Millie the Model Dating the Hulk?\" and \"I'm Tired of Standing Doing Nothing\" by the Watcher.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: Good old Watcher might have been able to use some of these in his classic <i>What If?</i> #34!&nbsp; <i>Hahahahaha!</i>]\n\nThe <b>Special Announcements Section</b> publishes the results of the \"Should we bring back Captain America?\" vote, which turned out to be overwhelmingly <b><i>yes</i></b>!&nbsp; And they say to stay tuned for <i>Avengers</i> 4 (AVEN #4), \"on sale about Jan. 3!&nbsp; And if you miss it, it's your own fault!\"\n\nIt also says to be on the lookout for a Thing vs Hulk issue next month!",
  "highlight": "Reed Richards, the Infant Terrible, and Big Joe combine forces to answer the question nobody asked:&nbsp; how much random nonsense can fit into a single comic book?"
}